              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "901c256e-ae7b-3a84-9d8e-1f1051909fbb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size argument passed to `malloc` is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to a buffer overflow or underflow. This can result in arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize input that is used to determine the size of the memory allocation.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns `NULL`, the memory allocation failed.\n3. Always initialize the allocated memory.\n4. Free the allocated memory when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming hPtr is a pointer to an integer\nint *hPtr = NULL;\n\n// Allocate memory\nhPtr = (int*)malloc(sizeof(*hPtr));\n\n// Check if memory allocation was successful\nif (hPtr == NULL) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n// Initialize memory\n*hPtr = 0;\n\n// Use hPtr...\n\n// Free allocated memory\nfree(hPtr);\nhPtr = NULL;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa2a0353-9d81-3a73-ad04-f4ca08d0f79d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc(size) and assigns the result to hOutputData, but there is no null check before the first use of this pointer in a memset call. This means that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the subsequent use of hOutputData could result in a null pointer dereference, leading to undefined behavior or a crash. There are no zero guards or overflow guards present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) without any prior validation. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, but these do not affect the immediate risk of null dereference. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning from the function, logging an error, or cleaning up resources). For example, after the allocation:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, or return error\n    return;\n}\n// Safe to use hOutputData now\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if memory allocation fails, the program does not attempt to dereference a NULL pointer, preventing undefined behavior or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "15845347-2032-3a7a-b16f-249bb8d5b692",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc(size) and assigns the result to hInputData, but there is no null check before the first use (memset at line 96). The absence of a zero guard, overflow guard, or null guard before use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, but these do not address the core vulnerability. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability where a failed allocation could lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting the operation). For example, after the allocation:\n\n```c\nhInputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hInputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, return error, etc.\n    return ERROR_CODE;\n}\n```\nThis ensures that if malloc fails, the program does not attempt to use a null pointer, preventing undefined behavior or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "88077c9c-ac5e-3f64-bd67-07f465990f62",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hData', which is then used as the destination in a memset call on the very next line, without any check to ensure that the allocation succeeded (i.e., that hData is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation that would dereference the pointer. This pattern is a classic source of null pointer dereference vulnerabilities, especially if malloc fails and returns NULL. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method, but this does not affect the risk of dereferencing a NULL pointer immediately after allocation. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hData, 0, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before using `hData`:\n\n```c\nhData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or cleanup\n    // ...\n} else {\n    memset(hData, 0, size);\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "63c48cd5-7ad7-3255-8487-bf129f2181bc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5e9a0558-0420-3750-8c73-c2679458d814",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
