            {
              "id": "4ff32a0f-d76d-30fa-a4ae-b012af412fec",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for the buffer size, causing the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input or external input that is used to determine the size of memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated, such as `calloc()`.\n3. Implement error handling for `malloc()`. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer. Always check if the returned pointer is null before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to fix this vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// ... \n\nsize_t buffer_size = get_buffer_size(); // get_buffer_size() is a hypothetical function\n\n// Validate buffer_size before using it\nif (buffer_size > MAX_BUFFER_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\nvoid* buffer = malloc(buffer_size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Use buffer\n\nfree(buffer);\n```\n\nIn this example, `MAX_BUFFER_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowable buffer size.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d05700c8-d1c5-33c0-bc23-f9602a6ff48f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\nIn the given code snippet `malloc(sizeof(hipStream_t))`, the vulnerability could arise if the size of `hipStream_t` is not properly controlled or if the return value of `malloc` is not checked for null (which indicates that the memory allocation failed).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc` for null. If `malloc` returns null, it means that the memory allocation failed. Ignoring this can lead to null pointer dereferencing which can crash your program.\n\n2. Be careful with the size of the memory you are trying to allocate. If the size is user-controlled or can be influenced by an attacker, it can lead to buffer overflows or memory corruption.\n\n3. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written such as `calloc` or `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nhipStream_t* ptr = (hipStream_t*) malloc(sizeof(hipStream_t));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    // use ptr\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we check if `malloc` returns null and handle the error appropriately. We also make sure to free the allocated memory after we are done using it to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet uses the `malloc` function which is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c7017125-60c8-34ff-af82-4398b956218a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int))`, there is no immediate vulnerability. However, if the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input or other untrusted data, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the size of the memory to be allocated. Never trust user input or other untrusted data.\n2. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Use modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) to manage dynamic memory, which can help prevent memory leaks and other related issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input, validate and sanitize the input. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
