                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9eb74e37-0361-3232-a5ed-ee419213e6d9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. In this case, the `memmove()` function is used, which does not check the boundaries of the buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include boundary checks. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For example, instead of `memmove()`, you can use `std::copy()` or `std::copy_backward()` from `<algorithm>` library, which are safer as they work with STL containers that have boundary checks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memmove()` function with `std::copy()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <sys/socket.h>\n\n// ...\n\nstruct cmsghdr* cmptr;\nint sendfd;\n\n// ...\n\nstd::copy(&sendfd, &sendfd + sizeof(sendfd), CMSG_DATA(cmptr));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` or `<cstring>` for `memmove()`\n- `<sys/socket.h>` for `CMSG_DATA()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3c93bd93-f312-3255-acdc-f1fb40d03eba",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an attacker, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust user input for memory allocation without validation. Ensure that the size of the memory to be allocated is within acceptable limits.\n\n2. Error handling: Always check if `malloc()` was successful by checking if the returned pointer is `NULL`.\n\n3. Use safer functions: If possible, use safer alternatives to `malloc()` like `calloc()`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#define MAX_WIDTH 1000\n\nvoid function(size_t width) {\n    if (width > MAX_WIDTH) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(width);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Use ptr\n\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we first check if the width is greater than a predefined maximum value. If it is, we handle the error and return. Then we call `malloc()` and check if the returned pointer is `NULL`. If it is, we handle the error and return. Finally, we use the allocated memory and free it when we're done.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the standard C library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "146d4a02-0a5d-3793-bbb5-403ffe370c58",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(size)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory on the heap. The size of the block is specified by the `size` parameter. If the allocation is successful, the function returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any data.\n\nA vulnerability can occur when the `size` parameter passed to `malloc` is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities, including buffer overflows, integer overflows, and memory corruption. These vulnerabilities can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, cause a denial of service, or leak sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and limit the `size` parameter passed to `malloc`.\n- Check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the allocation was successful.\n- Initialize the allocated memory to a known value using `memset` or similar function.\n- Use a safe version of `malloc` that includes built-in error checking and validation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to safely use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    if (size == 0 || size > SIZE_MAX) {\n        // Invalid size\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Allocation failed\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    // Initialize memory to zero\n    memset(ptr, 0, size);\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: for `malloc`\n- `string.h`: for `memset`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "96ab1af4-ba3b-370c-935b-0c88ab840e6d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to serious security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(cliaddr.sun_path, temp, sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1);\ncliaddr.sun_path[sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code ensures that no more than `sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1` bytes are copied from `temp` to `cliaddr.sun_path`, and that the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` for the `strncpy` function\n- `<sys/un.h>` for the `sockaddr_un` structure\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "002235c5-6e6e-368c-b1c1-b35094b937cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(handle->name, name, sizeof(handle->name) - 1);\nhandle->name[sizeof(handle->name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `name` to `handle->name`, but it will not copy more than `sizeof(handle->name) - 1` characters. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5721bdfd-b3b3-3cfd-8ce5-efa22633f646",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used properly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen(name) + 1` is used to allocate memory for `handle->name`. If the length of `name` is not properly checked, it can lead to buffer overflow, as more data than allocated can be written into `handle->name`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the length of the string before using it in memory allocation. Also, consider using safer functions that limit the amount of data written to the buffer, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t length = strlen(name);\nif (length > MAX_LENGTH) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    handle->name = new char[length + 1];\n    strncpy(handle->name, name, length);\n    handle->name[length] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowed length for `name`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strlen` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6087c1ee-6c50-3e0a-8b1e-79023508dcea",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, application crashes, and other serious security issues. In this case, the `memmove()` function is used, which does not perform any bounds checking on the source buffer, making it a potential source of buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these insecure C library functions. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For instance, instead of using `memmove()`, you can use `std::copy()` or `std::copy_backward()` from the `<algorithm>` library, which are safer as they work with STL containers that provide bounds checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memmove()` function with `std::copy()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::copy(CMSG_DATA(cmptr), CMSG_DATA(cmptr) + sizeof(receivedfd), &receivedfd);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sys/socket.h>`: For the `CMSG_DATA()` macro.\n- `<algorithm>`: For the `std::copy()` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b30c9133-4b1f-35a2-a1b4-e2b2cb44cbc2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to serious security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, and information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(cliaddr.sun_path, name, sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1);\ncliaddr.sun_path[sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code ensures that no more than `sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1` bytes are copied from `name` to `cliaddr.sun_path`, and that the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` for the `strncpy` function\n- `<sys/un.h>` for the `sockaddr_un` structure\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
