            {
              "id": "f7c181a6-b712-3d21-bb65-658ab99c0570",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to the value in the environment, or NULL if there is no match. The returned pointer might be invalidated or the associated string might be overwritten by a subsequent call to `std::getenv`, `std::setenv`, `std::putenv`, or `std::unsetenv`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function directly. Instead, use safer alternatives that are less prone to security vulnerabilities. Always validate and sanitize the input and output of these functions. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv` function in combination with `std::optional` to safely retrieve environment variables:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <optional>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::optional<std::string> get_env(const char* name) {\n    if(const char* env_p = std::getenv(name)) {\n        return std::string{env_p};\n    }\n    return {};\n}\n\n// Usage\nauto val = get_env(kEnvarName);\nif(val.has_value()) {\n    // Use val.value() safely\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n- `<optional>`: This header file contains template class optional and its supporting functions.\n\n- `<string>`: This header file contains std::string class and its supporting functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
