            {
              "id": "df2cf880-a3c8-34b8-b593-57d72933ff54",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `vfprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if it can be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the ones provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you could use `std::vfprintf` with a format string that you control, so it cannot be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::vfprintf`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid safe_vfprintf(const char* fmt, ...) {\n    va_list ap;\n    va_start(ap, fmt);\n    std::vfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n    va_end(ap);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdarg>`: for handling variable argument lists.\n- `<cstdio>`: for `std::vfprintf` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
