            {
              "id": "a5ab2c81-8a4b-3303-9dbb-45c8c80f8abd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `buffer_size_new` variable is controlled by the user or can be manipulated, it can lead to a buffer overflow or underflow. This can cause the program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n- Always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust data provided by the user.\n- Use functions that limit the amount of data written into a buffer.\n- Avoid using functions that do not check buffer sizes such as `strcpy`, `sprintf`, `gets`, etc.\n- Use modern C++ features and libraries that manage memory automatically, such as smart pointers and STL containers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Check if buffer_size_new is a reasonable size\nif (buffer_size_new > 0 && buffer_size_new <= MAX_BUFFER_SIZE) {\n    void* buffer = malloc(buffer_size_new);\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    free(buffer);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `stdlib.h`: This library is required for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9f02c472-b30f-3d9b-af2b-a4fac151127d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if the size of `T` is larger than the remaining space in `dst` starting from `dst + tid`, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer and the size of the data before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(T) <= (sizeof(dst) - tid)) {\n    memcpy(dst + tid, src + tid, sizeof(T));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if the size of `T` is less than or equal to the remaining space in `dst` starting from `dst + tid`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it is not, we handle the error in a way that is appropriate for the specific application.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
