            {
              "id": "3be10384-bdea-336a-a464-595369669d07",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated incorrectly, leading to a buffer overflow or underflow. This can lead to unexpected behavior, crashes, or even security vulnerabilities such as code execution or information disclosure.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the vulnerability arises if `buffer_size_new` is less than `buffer_size`. This will result in a negative value being passed to `malloc`, which will then return a null pointer or potentially allocate a large amount of memory due to integer underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size argument passed to `malloc` is non-negative. This can be done by checking the values of `buffer_size_new` and `buffer_size` before the `malloc` call. If `buffer_size_new` is less than `buffer_size`, you should handle this case appropriately, for example by returning an error or allocating a default minimum size.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```c++\nif (buffer_size_new < buffer_size) {\n    // Handle error appropriately\n    // For example, return or throw an exception\n    return;\n}\n\nvoid* buffer = malloc(buffer_size_new - buffer_size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error appropriately\n    // For example, return or throw an exception\n    return;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
