            {
              "id": "4375c627-ebb5-3585-a021-8ba0b5ee7b86",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from a source address to a destination address. A vulnerability can occur when the size of the source buffer is larger than the destination buffer, leading to a buffer overflow. This can result in unexpected behavior, including data corruption, crashes, or code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nif (alloc_size <= sizeof(dst)) {\n    memcpy(dst, src, alloc_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the size of the destination buffer (`sizeof(dst)`) is larger than or equal to the size of the data we want to copy (`alloc_size`). If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it's not, we handle the error in a way that is appropriate for the specific program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible. Always refer to the latest OWASP and CWE documentation for the most accurate and up-to-date information."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2cffa9b8-aba5-37b1-9eff-6080d870e6a3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fc43ccaa-48f5-3519-b383-2ddf13b08904",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory through data flow, which can cause undefined behavior when using memory copy operations like memcpy. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer may be null at this location, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. The destination is not likely to be null, but the overlap and null source risks make this a high-confidence issue. No explicit bounds or null-termination protections are present, and the size argument is not derived from the destination's capacity, further increasing the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap when using memcpy. If there is any possibility of overlap, use memmove instead, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```c\nif (src != NULL && dst != NULL && !regions_overlap(dst, src, size)) {\n    memcpy(dst, src, size);\n} else if (src != NULL && dst != NULL) {\n    memmove(dst, src, size);\n}\n```\n\nHere, `regions_overlap` is a placeholder for logic that determines if the memory regions overlap. If overlap is possible, memmove is used instead of memcpy. Always validate pointers before use.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9ec350e7-2d5f-3e26-ba89-cf3a4254037a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for the standard memory copy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer may be null at this point in the code, as there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The destination pointer is less likely to be null, but the main concern is the potential for overlapping memory regions and null source pointer, both of which can lead to serious runtime errors or security vulnerabilities. No explicit bounds or null-termination checks are present, and the count argument is not derived from the destination's capacity, further increasing the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use a memory move function that safely handles overlapping regions. Additionally, always check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy operation. For example:\n\n```c\nif (src != NULL && dst != NULL && !regions_overlap(dst, src, size)) {\n    memcpy(dst, src, size);\n} else if (src != NULL && dst != NULL) {\n    memmove(dst, src, size); // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n} else {\n    // Handle null pointer error\n}\n```\n\nImplement a helper function `regions_overlap` to check for overlap:\n\n```c\nbool regions_overlap(const void* dst, const void* src, size_t size) {\n    const char* d = (const char*)dst;\n    const char* s = (const char*)src;\n    return (d < s + size) && (s < d + size);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "82b69e50-0562-325e-a5a1-37c7c7cf714f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for the memcpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent the source from being null, which further increases the risk. The destination is not likely to be null, but the combination of possible overlap and a potentially null source makes this a high-confidence issue that should be addressed. The destination type is not a pointer, and the size argument is not derived from the destination's capacity, which means there are no mitigating factors present.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. The `memmove` function is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(dst, src, size);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that the `src` pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid a null pointer dereference:\n\n```c\nif (src != NULL) {\n    memmove(dst, src, size);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6e501d3d-c91b-3f11-8f82-9cebe7e0e64d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. The `fopen` function is one of these prohibited functions. It is used to open a file, but it does not check for errors that could lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these prohibited functions. For `fopen`, a safer alternative could be `fopen_s` which is a part of the C11 standard. This function includes error handling and is therefore less likely to lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with `fopen_s`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\nFILE* file;\nerrno_t err = fopen_s(&file, \"file.txt\", \"r\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use file\n    fclose(file);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdio.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ad384a3c-a29a-325e-a067-b65bdcba3c7d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. In this case, the `memmove()` function is used, which does not check the boundaries of the buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include boundary checks. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For example, instead of `memmove()`, you can use `std::copy()` or `std::copy_backward()` from `<algorithm>` library, which are safer as they work with STL containers that have boundary checks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memmove()` function with `std::copy()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <sys/socket.h>\n\n// ...\n\nstruct cmsghdr* cmptr;\nint sendfd;\n\n// ...\n\nstd::copy(&sendfd, &sendfd + sizeof(sendfd), CMSG_DATA(cmptr));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` or `<cstring>` for `memmove()`\n- `<sys/socket.h>` for `CMSG_DATA()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "52e46c6b-1e6a-35c0-ad08-ef63de29845a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to serious security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(cliaddr.sun_path, temp, sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1);\ncliaddr.sun_path[sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code ensures that no more than `sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1` bytes are copied from `temp` to `cliaddr.sun_path`, and that the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` for the `strncpy` function\n- `<sys/un.h>` for the `sockaddr_un` structure\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3f518aa2-9cb9-3c8f-bca3-7570ee19725c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for the standard memory copy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer may be null at this point in the code, as there is no prior check ensuring it is valid. The destination pointer is less likely to be null, but the primary concern is the potential for overlapping memory regions and null source pointer, both of which can lead to serious runtime errors or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use a memory move function that is designed to handle overlapping regions. Additionally, always check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy operation. For example:\n\n```c\nif (src != NULL && dst != NULL && !regions_overlap(dst, src, size)) {\n    memcpy(dst, src, size);\n} else if (src != NULL && dst != NULL) {\n    memmove(dst, src, size); // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n}\n```\n\nImplement a helper function `regions_overlap` to check for overlap if needed. Always validate pointers before use to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f7df25fe-0385-3486-843c-f1259c34301f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(handle->name, name, sizeof(handle->name) - 1);\nhandle->name[sizeof(handle->name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `name` to `handle->name`, but it will not copy more than `sizeof(handle->name) - 1` characters. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "83ad8a2f-c1ec-3e12-b8c4-fb28ed4634ae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used properly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen(name) + 1` is used to allocate memory for `handle->name`. If the length of `name` is not properly checked, it can lead to buffer overflow, as more data than allocated can be written into `handle->name`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the length of the string before using it in memory allocation. Also, consider using safer functions that limit the amount of data written to the buffer, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t length = strlen(name);\nif (length > MAX_LENGTH) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    handle->name = new char[length + 1];\n    strncpy(handle->name, name, length);\n    handle->name[length] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowed length for `name`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strlen` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d76b8717-16d6-36b7-9384-cbef0c197317",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, application crashes, and other serious security issues. In this case, the `memmove()` function is used, which does not perform any bounds checking on the source buffer, making it a potential source of buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these insecure C library functions. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For instance, instead of using `memmove()`, you can use `std::copy()` or `std::copy_backward()` from the `<algorithm>` library, which are safer as they work with STL containers that provide bounds checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memmove()` function with `std::copy()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::copy(CMSG_DATA(cmptr), CMSG_DATA(cmptr) + sizeof(receivedfd), &receivedfd);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sys/socket.h>`: For the `CMSG_DATA()` macro.\n- `<algorithm>`: For the `std::copy()` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f9ee9cd-0fda-333c-9710-f9980fdc8667",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to serious security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, and information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(cliaddr.sun_path, name, sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1);\ncliaddr.sun_path[sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code ensures that no more than `sizeof(cliaddr.sun_path) - 1` bytes are copied from `name` to `cliaddr.sun_path`, and that the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string.h>` for the `strncpy` function\n- `<sys/un.h>` for the `sockaddr_un` structure\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d7c6f6a4-b68d-302d-826c-58af23cf8bb6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used properly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen(name) + 1` is used to allocate memory for `handle->name`. If the length of `name` is not properly checked, it can lead to buffer overflow, as more data than allocated can be written into `handle->name`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the length of the string before using it in memory allocation. Also, consider using safer functions that limit the amount of data written to the buffer, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t length = strlen(name);\nif (length > MAX_LENGTH) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    handle->name = new char[length + 1];\n    strncpy(handle->name, name, length);\n    handle->name[length] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowed length for `name`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strlen` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
