            {
              "id": "acbb1b88-3cdb-3d54-aecc-1cb015a03b44",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from a source address to a destination address. A vulnerability can occur when the size of the source buffer and the size of the destination buffer are not checked to ensure they are the same. This can lead to a buffer overflow, where data is written beyond the end of the destination buffer, potentially leading to code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the size of the buffers before copying. This can be done by comparing the size of the source buffer and the destination buffer and ensuring that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source buffer's data.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer functions that include buffer size checks, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` (in C11), instead of `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(dst, src, size);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size <= sizeof(dst)) {\n    memcpy(dst, src, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nOr use a safer function:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(dst, src, sizeof(dst));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-120)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
