            {
              "id": "41d8467c-abdc-338e-91d3-af5e764c9fb5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int))`, there is no immediate vulnerability. However, if the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input or other untrusted data, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the size of the memory to be allocated. Never trust user input or other untrusted data.\n2. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Use modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) to manage dynamic memory, which can help prevent memory leaks and other related issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input, validate and sanitize the input. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
