            {
              "id": "0530bcb8-75ff-3d65-89be-a4d848d328ea",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of an untrusted input or a calculation that can be manipulated, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\nThe specific sink in the provided code is `devcount*sizeof(hipGraphExec_t)`. If `devcount` is controlled by an attacker or can be manipulated, it can lead to the aforementioned issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and sanitize inputs. Never trust user input blindly.\n- Use functions that limit the amount of data written into a buffer.\n- Avoid using functions that do not check bounds.\n- Use modern memory management techniques and libraries that automatically manage memory for you.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, a general recommendation would be to add checks to ensure `devcount` is within expected bounds before using it in `malloc`.\n\n```cpp\nif (devcount > 0 && devcount <= MAX_DEVCOUNT) {\n    malloc(devcount*sizeof(hipGraphExec_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nReplace `MAX_DEVCOUNT` with the maximum value `devcount` should have in your context.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using the HIP library, which is a C++ Runtime API and Kernel Language that allows developers to create portable GPU-accelerated applications.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
