              "id": "300bdfe1-cb95-3a9d-8ba9-7c47dd88341b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not correctly calculated, leading to buffer overflow or underflow errors. In the provided code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int*))` is allocating memory of size equal to the size of an integer pointer, which may not be the intended size for the data to be stored. This can lead to memory corruption, data leakage, or even execution of arbitrary code if exploited.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always calculate the size of the memory to be allocated correctly. Consider the type and number of elements to be stored.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns `NULL`, the memory allocation failed.\n3. Always free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you intend to allocate memory for an integer, use `sizeof(int)` instead of `sizeof(int*)`. If you want to allocate memory for multiple integers, multiply the size of an integer by the number of elements.\n\n```c++\nint* ptr = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int)); // where n is the number of elements\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle memory allocation failure\n}\n// use ptr\nfree(ptr); // free memory after use\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
