            {
              "id": "ba744ef0-909c-3f40-ba4f-2f3f5e28b286",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as `devcount*sizeof(hipGraph_t)`. If `devcount` is a user-controlled input and not properly validated, it can lead to the allocation of an unexpected amount of memory, causing a potential security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate user-controlled inputs. Ensure that the value of `devcount` is within the expected range before using it to calculate the size of the memory to be allocated.\n\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero and can help prevent certain types of vulnerabilities.\n\n3. Consider using modern C++ features, such as smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`), or container classes (`std::vector`, `std::array`), which handle memory management automatically and are less prone to memory-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming devcount is a user-controlled input\nif (devcount > MAX_DEVCOUNT || devcount < 0) {\n    // Handle error: devcount is out of range\n} else {\n    hipGraph_t* graphArray = static_cast<hipGraph_t*>(calloc(devcount, sizeof(hipGraph_t)));\n    if (graphArray == nullptr) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use graphArray\n        // ...\n        free(graphArray);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library for the `calloc` and `free` functions, and the `hip/hip_runtime_api.h` library for the `hipGraph_t` type.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
