              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the given code snippet `malloc( N * sizeof(char)`, if the value of `N` is not properly validated, it can lead to these issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of memory to be allocated. Ensure that it is within the acceptable range and not a negative value.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of data written into a buffer such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features and libraries that manage memory automatically, such as smart pointers or container classes like `std::vector`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Check if N is within acceptable range\nif (N > 0 && N <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(N * sizeof(char));\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    free(buffer);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e2509b08-3358-3553-a59e-87d89add6341",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9cffcf44-eb44-3bdc-addf-d8da88a839c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for the `byte_size` parameter, causing the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. Additionally, you should always check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how you can validate the size of the memory to be allocated and check the return value of `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t byte_size) {\n    if (byte_size <= 0 || byte_size > MAX_SIZE) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(byte_size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum allowable size for your program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the `stdlib.h` library in C++, so this library must be included in your program for the `malloc()` function to work.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "619c32a7-b8dc-3b81-a807-c64de96b02e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for the `byte_size` parameter, causing the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. Additionally, you should always check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how you can validate the size of the memory to be allocated and check the return value of `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t byte_size) {\n    if (byte_size <= 0 || byte_size > MAX_SIZE) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(byte_size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum allowable size for your program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the `stdlib.h` library in C++, so this library must be included in your program for the `malloc()` function to work.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
